          "source": "def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Write $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm $\\log$ of [[def-natural-logarithm]]. Unless a base is displayed, every information quantity on this page uses $\\ln$ and is measured in **nats**. For a base $b>1$ and $t>0$, define\n$$\\log_b t:=\\frac{\\ln t}{\\ln b}.$$\nThe denominator is positive: $b>1$, $\\ln$ is strictly increasing, and $\\ln 1=0$ by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]. A base-$b$ entropy or divergence is obtained by replacing each $\\ln$ in the finite formulas below by $\\log_b$; use $0\\log_b0:=0$ and assign $+\\infty$ to a positive numerator over a zero denominator. The case $b=2$ measures **bits**; $b=e$ gives nats.",
