        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite nonempty set identified with $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, a **probability vector** is $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^k$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; it is the outcome-weight vector of a finite probability space ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]). Its Shannon entropy in natural units is\n$$H(p):=-\\sum_{i=1}^k p_i\\ln p_i,$$\nwith $0\\ln0:=0$. For probability vectors $p,q$ on the same set $[k]$, the relative entropy is\n$$D(p\\|q):=\\sum_{i:p_i>0}p_i\\ln(p_i/q_i),$$\nwith value $+\\infty$ if $p_i>0=q_i$ for some $i$. A matrix $A\\in[0,1]^{N\\times m}$ is **row-stochastic** when each row sums to one. For a probability row vector $p$ on $[m]$ and a row vector $X\\in[0,1]^m$, the dot product $pX^{\\mathsf T}=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_iX_i$ is the acceptance probability under distribution $p$.\n\nThese definitions use finite sets only; entropy is between $0$ and $\\ln k$, as proved where needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-finite-source-information-density-and-typical-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For the finite-alphabet IID source in\n[[def-finite-alphabet-iid-source]], with one-letter law $p$ and block law\n$p^n$ from [[def-finite-law-self-information-and-entropy]], let $X^n$ have\nthat law. Write $H(p)$ for the finite entropy in\n[[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]]. On the\npositive-probability support $A_+^n$, define normalized block information\ndensity using the natural-log/nats convention from\n[[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]]\n$$I_n(x^n):=-\\frac1n\\ln p^n(x^n).$$\nFor $\\varepsilon>0$, its epsilon-typical set is\n$$T_n(\\varepsilon):=\\{x^n\\in A_+^n:|I_n(x^n)-H(p)|\\le\\varepsilon\\}.$$\nSequences of zero probability are excluded, so the definition never takes a\nlogarithm of zero.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "cor-iid-finite-variance-weak-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_k)_{k\\ge1}$ be IID square-integrable real random variables, with $\\mu=\\mathbb EX_1$ and $\\sigma^2=\\operatorname{Var}(X_1)$. For $S_n=\\sum_{k=1}^nX_k$,\n$$\\mathbb E|S_n/n-\\mu|^2=\\sigma^2/n,$$\nand $S_n/n\\to\\mu$ in $L^2$ and in probability. Also $\\mathbb P(|S_n/n-\\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\sigma^2/(n\\varepsilon^2)$ for $\\varepsilon>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
